Young Milo had a wart so round,  
The proudest little wart in town.  

His mother rubbed it twice with cheese;  
The wart just sighed and said, “More, please.”  

His father tied it up with string;  
By dawn, it wore the knot like bling.  

The doctor froze it hard as ice;  
The wart came back exactly twice.  

A wizard waved a smoking sock;  
The wart grew hands and checked the clock.  

They soaked it deep in pickle brine;  
It burped and said, “The taste is fine.”  

They tried a laser, bright and hot;  
It burned the chair—but not the spot.  

They fired a cannon with a spark;  
The blast was heard beyond the park.  

The roof flew west, the door flew right;  
The cat took off in shocked delight.  

At last the smoke thinned out at dawn;  
The wart remained—but Milo was gone.
